36,2,1,talk,Midori,,My findings are concerning. We've had issues with support ownership during outages and a lack of flexibility when business rules change mid-year. We need a vendor that offers clear accountability and adaptable configurations.,"{""words"":33,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
37,2,1,talk,Ryuji,,"My findings confirm my fears. Our depots have no freeze window during peak season, and our hardware is a fragmented mix of old models. Any system that can't handle a live cutover or outdated devices will fail.","{""words"":37,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
38,2,1,talk,May,,"My findings are just as grim. We've skipped critical upgrades because we lacked the time to test, and external data migrations have left us with lingering errors. We cannot afford another ""black box"" implementation.","{""words"":34,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
39,2,1,talk,Misaki,,"The human side is just as fragile. We are losing key administrators and struggling to hire replacements, while depot supervisors tend to ignore standard training in favor of their own routines. We need a system that is easy to maintain and adopt.","{""words"":42,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
40,2,1,talk,Benjamin,,"It appears we are all uncovering a rather consistent theme of fragility. Financially, our board favors operating expenses over capital, and fragmented licenses are bleeding us dry. We need a predictable cost structure, not a series of escalating surprises.","{""words"":39,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
41,2,2,talk,Midori,,"@Benjamin, if fragmented licenses are the problem, we should avoid modular systems with separate contracts. @Ryuji, given the hardware and peak-season issues, do you think an on-site team or a hosted solution would be safer?","{""words"":35,""mentions"":[""Benjamin"",""Ryuji""],""raw_differs"":false}"
42,2,2,talk,Ryuji,,"@Midori, an on-site team is preferable. We cannot risk a remote rollout that ignores our hardware reality or crashes during peak season. We need someone physically present to ensure the system survives the yard.","{""words"":34,""mentions"":[""Midori""],""raw_differs"":false}"
43,2,2,talk,May,,"@Ryuji, I agree on the presence, but not if it means we're stuck with a system that's impossible to upgrade. We need a vendor that doesn't just install it and leave us to rot.","{""words"":34,""mentions"":[""Ryuji""],""raw_differs"":false}"
44,2,2,talk,Misaki,,"@May, that's a valid point. Given our hiring struggles, we can't rely on a complex system that requires rare skills to maintain. We need something intuitive that doesn't collapse when a few key people retire.","{""words"":35,""mentions"":[""May""],""raw_differs"":false}"
45,2,2,talk,Benjamin,,"@Midori, your intuition is spot on. A modular approach sounds like a recipe for financial attrition. We need a bundled, predictable cost model to avoid those creeping renewal fees that the board seems to ignore.","{""words"":35,""mentions"":[""Midori""],""raw_differs"":false}"
46,2,3,talk,Midori,,It seems we are all leaning toward a solution that is integrated and well-supported. We need a vendor that provides a single point of accountability and a predictable cost structure to avoid the chaos we've seen.,"{""words"":36,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
47,2,3,talk,Ryuji,,"@Midori, exactly. To survive our environment, we need a partner who understands the physical reality of the depot and provides a cohesive system, rather than a collection of parts that might clash during a peak-season rush.","{""words"":36,""mentions"":[""Midori""],""raw_differs"":false}"
48,2,3,talk,May,,"And we must ensure the implementation isn't just a quick fix by an external team. If we're handing over the keys, we need a vendor that guarantees the system stays current without requiring a month of downtime for every update.","{""words"":40,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
49,2,3,talk,Misaki,,"I agree. If the system is too rigid or complex, our supervisors will just revert to their old ways, and our remaining staff will be overwhelmed. We need a solution that is sustainable for the people actually running it.","{""words"":39,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
50,2,3,talk,Benjamin,,"It seems we are in rare agreement. We require a partner who offers a unified cost structure, a cohesive system to avoid finger-pointing, and a deployment strategy that respects both our hardware and our humans.","{""words"":35,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
51,2,4,talk,Midori,,"I think we've defined our criteria well. We need a single point of accountability, a predictable cost model, and a deployment that respects our operational reality. I'm ready to see the candidates and make a choice.","{""words"":36,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
52,2,4,talk,Ryuji,,I agree. We have a clear set of requirements now. I will be looking for a candidate that can realistically handle our hardware and the lack of a freeze window. I am ready.,"{""words"":33,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
53,2,4,talk,May,,"I'm ready too. I'll be looking for a vendor that avoids the ""install and forget"" trap and ensures we aren't left cleaning up a mess of bad data or outdated software.","{""words"":31,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
54,2,4,talk,Misaki,,I am also ready. I will prioritize a vendor that minimizes the burden on our shrinking administrative team and provides training that actually sticks with the depot crews.,"{""words"":28,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
55,2,4,talk,Benjamin,,"I shall join you in that readiness. I will be scanning for a vendor whose pricing is transparent and bundled, ensuring our long-term costs don't become a ghost that haunts our budget.","{""words"":32,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
